      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die, we can break the problem into two separate events and use the basic principle of probability for independent events.\n\n1. **Probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die:**\n   A six-sided die has the numbers 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers less than three are 1 and 2. So, there are 2 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{number less than 3 on the first die}) = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die:**\n   The numbers greater than three on a six-sided die are 4, 5, and 6. So, there are 3 favorable outcomes out of 6 possible outcomes.\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{number greater than 3 on the second die}) = \\frac{3}{6} = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n\nSince the rolls of the two dice are independent, we can find the combined probability by multiplying the probabilities of the two individual events.\n\\[\nP(\\text{number less than 3 on the first die and number greater than 3 on the second die}) = P(\\text{number less than 3 on the first die}) \\times P(\\text{number greater than 3 on the second die}) = \\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
